eating a horizontal wall of my pieces at D4-D5-D6. While stable, this formation allows the opponent easy access to the C-column (C3, C4, C5, C6), giving them high mobility (4 options).\n2.  **(F, 6)**: This move flips the central piece E5 diagonally. This creates a scattered formation and opens up several lines for the opponent (D3, E3, E7, F5, G6), resulting in high opponent mobility (5 options).\n3.  **(F, 4)**: This move flips E4 vertically. It keeps the piece formation relatively compact and limits the opponent's valid responses to roughly 3 moves (D3, E3, F3).\n\nReason: In Othello/Reversi, a key opening strategy is to minimize the opponent's mobility (number of valid moves). Move (F, 4) grants the opponent the fewest options compared to the other available moves, allowing me to better control the board's development.\n\nAnswer: (F, 4)"}
