s current cluster. It seems like it creates new opportunities for them. If I play C7, the opponent might respond with E7 and B6. That\'s not the game I want to play.\n\nSo, it\'s about mobility and control. E7 is safer, strategically smarter, and less likely to accidentally open me up to a quick counterattack. So, after a long analysis, E7 seems to be the best move. It\'s not the flashy move, but it is solid, strategic, and sets up a controlled game.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is currently in the middle phase. The opponent (\'*\') has established a solid block of pieces primarily on the C and D rows, while my pieces (\'@\') are positioned on the D, E, and F rows. The valid moves available to me are concentrated on the B-column (left edge of the cluster) and the 7th row (bottom edge). The goal is to maximize stability and limit the opponent\'s future mobility.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Moves on the B-column (B3, B4, B5, B6) are "loud" moves that open up the empty left side of the board, creating a large frontier that gives the opponent many counter-play options. B2 is specifically an X-square move, which is strategically dangerous as it often concedes the adjacent corner (A1) to the opponent.\n- Move (D, 7) flips four pieces (D3, D4, D5, D6). While it captures many pieces, it creates a long wall of my color in the center, which serves as a massive target for the opponent to play against, essentially increasing their mobility.\n- Moves (C, 7) and (E, 7) are "quiet moves" that flip only one piece each (D6 and E6, respectively). Quiet moves are generally superior in this phase to maintain structural advantage.\n- Comparing (C, 7) and (E, 7): Playing (C, 7) flips the internal piece D6, which opens up lines for the opponent to play moves like B6 or E7. Playing (E, 7) flips E6 and consolidates the E-column. It leaves the opponent with fewer strong responses, effectively restricting their play to the F-column.\n\nReason: I chose (E, 7) because it is the most stable "quiet move" available. It flips only one opponent piece, minimizing the exposure of my own pieces (frontier). By playing (E, 7), I limit the opponent\'s available responses more effectively than with (C, 7) or the expansive B-column moves, thereby preserving a mobility advantage.\n\nAnswer: (E, 7)'}
