ey immediately take D1. Doesn\'t seem stable.\n*   **(H, 2, H, 5, H, 6):** These are edge moves, which have their own risks and rewards. H2 is a C-square near H1 (empty, so it\'s a bit dangerous). H5 and H6 have potential, but I have to calculate the implications carefully. Specifically, I\'m watching to not open too many moves for the opponent.\n\n**Comparing Key Options and Making a Decision**\n\nI have to choose between B6 and H6. B6 feels right. B6 allows B6 to have solid support with a strong wall (C6, D6, E5-E7). This will give me a better chance of keeping the opponent at bay. H6 also has potential, but the edge is more volatile. I think by choosing B6, I\'m more likely to control the board. I don\'t give the opponent obvious moves and I\'m blocking the opponent in a limited space. So the obvious choice here is B6.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n(B, 6)\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the middle phase. The opponent controls the left side of the center (columns 2-5, rows C-E), while I control the right side (columns 6-7, rows C-F). The top (rows A, B) and bottom (row H) are largely empty. My goal is to limit the opponent\'s mobility and avoid giving away corners.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(B, 1)**: This is a C-square move next to the empty corner A1. It would allow the opponent to immediately capture the A1 corner (A1 -> B1 -> C1), which is a major strategic error.\n- **(B, 2)**: This is an X-square move adjacent to corner A1. It creates a structural weakness that the opponent might exploit to take the corner later.\n- **(D, 1)**: This is an edge move. While it flips several discs, it is unstable because the opponent can likely recapture the edge (e.g., via E1), leaving me with a weak position.\n- **(B, 6)**: This is a "quiet move." It flips only one disc (C6), turning it from opponent\'s to mine. This strengthens my control of the center-right block without opening up new attack lines for the opponent. It does not offer the opponent access to corners.\n- **(H, 